1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,093
A person with diabetes sees their
doctor maybe four times a year.

2
00:00:07,143 --> 00:00:13,521
In between those visits, their blood sugar
rises and falls hundreds of times a day,

3
00:00:13,571 --> 00:00:15,236
and nobody is watching.

4
00:00:15,286 --> 00:00:20,379
Now imagine every patient had a
virtual copy of their own body.

5
00:00:20,429 --> 00:00:26,807
A copy that could warn the doctor about
a dangerous spike or a dangerous low,

6
00:00:26,857 --> 00:00:28,950
two hours before it happens.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,236
And a copy on which the
doctor could test advice,

8
00:00:33,286 --> 00:00:36,664
like a smaller dinner or a short walk,

9
00:00:36,714 --> 00:00:40,093
before giving that advice
to the real patient.

10
00:00:40,143 --> 00:00:42,236
That is what we built.

11
00:00:42,286 --> 00:00:44,379
This is Sugar Twin India,

12
00:00:44,429 --> 00:00:48,664
our entry for the Happiest Health
Digital Twin Challenge 2026.

13
00:00:50,714 --> 00:00:53,664
I am Aayush Gupta, the team leader,

14
00:00:53,714 --> 00:01:00,093
and I am presenting on behalf of our team
of three from Maharaja Agrasen Institute

15
00:01:00,143 --> 00:01:01,379
of Technology, Delhi.

16
00:01:01,429 --> 00:01:04,807
With me on this project are Dhruv Gandhi,

17
00:01:04,857 --> 00:01:09,521
who worked on the machine-learning
models and how we tested them,

18
00:01:09,571 --> 00:01:15,093
and Divyansh Singh, who worked on the
data, its validation and the documentation.

19
00:01:15,143 --> 00:01:18,950
I built the data simulator
and the doctor dashboard.

20
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,379
In the next twenty minutes I will show
you the problem, how our twin works,

21
00:01:25,429 --> 00:01:30,950
a live demo, and our results,
including the parts that did not work.

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,807
India has about 101 million
people living with diabetes,

23
00:01:36,857 --> 00:01:39,807
according to the
ICMR-INDIAB study from 2023.

24
00:01:39,857 --> 00:01:45,807
Most of them have Type 2 diabetes,
so that is the condition we chose.

25
00:01:45,857 --> 00:01:50,521
The real damage in diabetes
does not happen in the clinic.

26
00:01:50,571 --> 00:01:52,236
It happens between visits.

27
00:01:52,286 --> 00:01:55,236
A rice-heavy lunch
sends glucose above 250.

28
00:01:55,286 --> 00:01:58,664
A late dinner keeps it high all night.

29
00:01:58,714 --> 00:02:05,521
A wedding or a festival, a missed tablet,
a fever, all of these push sugar up.

30
00:02:05,571 --> 00:02:11,093
And for patients on sulfonylureas or
insulin, the opposite danger is a low,

31
00:02:11,143 --> 00:02:16,664
often early in the morning, which can
make someone dizzy, confused, or worse.

32
00:02:16,714 --> 00:02:19,236
Today, technology already
collects this data.

33
00:02:19,286 --> 00:02:24,379
A continuous glucose monitor, or CGM,
records a reading every five minutes.

34
00:02:24,429 --> 00:02:26,950
That is 288 readings a day.

35
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,379
A smartwatch adds heart
rate, sleep and steps.

36
00:02:30,429 --> 00:02:32,521
But this creates two problems.

37
00:02:32,571 --> 00:02:38,093
First, a doctor cannot read 288
numbers a day for hundreds of patients.

38
00:02:38,143 --> 00:02:41,093
Second, a CGM only shows the past.

39
00:02:41,143 --> 00:02:44,093
It tells you that glucose was high.

40
00:02:44,143 --> 00:02:49,236
It does not tell you that
it is about to be high.

41
00:02:49,286 --> 00:02:55,664
At the same time, the hospital record
knows a lot about the patient: their HbA1c,

42
00:02:55,714 --> 00:02:59,093
their kidney function, their
medicines, even genetic risk.

43
00:02:59,143 --> 00:03:04,664
But that record is not connected to
the live stream from the sensor.

44
00:03:04,714 --> 00:03:06,807
So the challenge is clear.

45
00:03:06,857 --> 00:03:10,236
Fuse the health record with the live data.

46
00:03:10,286 --> 00:03:12,807
Predict the event before it happens.

47
00:03:12,857 --> 00:03:18,379
And turn it into something a busy
doctor can act on in seconds.

48
00:03:20,429 --> 00:03:27,236
Our answer is a digital twin of each patient
that does three things for the doctor.

49
00:03:27,286 --> 00:03:28,436
One, triage.

50
00:03:28,486 --> 00:03:31,864
Every patient is sorted by risk right now.

51
00:03:31,914 --> 00:03:36,579
Urgent means a low, or a
reading above 250, is likely.

52
00:03:36,629 --> 00:03:40,007
Watch means a spike above 180 is likely.

53
00:03:40,057 --> 00:03:41,293
Two, early warning.

54
00:03:41,343 --> 00:03:48,579
For each patient, the twin predicts a spike
above 180, above 250, or a low below 70,

55
00:03:48,629 --> 00:03:53,293
up to two hours ahead, and
explains why in plain language.

56
00:03:53,343 --> 00:03:55,007
Three, advice tested first.

57
00:03:55,057 --> 00:04:00,579
The twin learns how this particular
patient's body responds to food and walking,

58
00:04:00,629 --> 00:04:06,150
so the doctor can try a change on
the twin before suggesting it.

59
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:11,579
Here is the whole system in one picture.

60
00:04:11,629 --> 00:04:12,864
On the left,

61
00:04:12,914 --> 00:04:18,436
the two data streams: a synthetic health
record for five thousand Indian patients,

62
00:04:18,486 --> 00:04:23,579
and fourteen days of CGM and
smartwatch data for each of them.

63
00:04:23,629 --> 00:04:24,864
In the middle,

64
00:04:24,914 --> 00:04:30,436
every thirty minutes we fuse both
streams into one row of 97 features,

65
00:04:30,486 --> 00:04:35,150
using only what a real app
would know at that moment.

66
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:36,864
Nothing from the future.

67
00:04:36,914 --> 00:04:40,293
Those features go into
two kinds of models.

68
00:04:40,343 --> 00:04:43,721
Machine-learning models, LightGBM
and a GRU neural network,

69
00:04:43,771 --> 00:04:45,864
predict what will happen next.

70
00:04:45,914 --> 00:04:47,150
Next to them,

71
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:52,721
a personal physiology twin learns how this
patient's body works and answers what-if

72
00:04:52,771 --> 00:04:53,921
questions.

73
00:04:53,971 --> 00:04:55,207
On the right,

74
00:04:55,257 --> 00:05:01,636
a FastAPI service and a React dashboard
put it all in front of the doctor.

75
00:05:03,686 --> 00:05:10,064
Let me start with an important point about
the data: every patient in this project

76
00:05:10,114 --> 00:05:11,264
is synthetic.

77
00:05:11,314 --> 00:05:16,407
Real patient data is protected by
India's DPDP Act and by HIPAA,

78
00:05:16,457 --> 00:05:20,264
and the challenge asks for
synthetic or open data.

79
00:05:20,314 --> 00:05:23,264
So we built our own population, carefully,

80
00:05:23,314 --> 00:05:26,264
and then checked it
against real-world numbers.

81
00:05:26,314 --> 00:05:29,264
The static stream is the health record.

82
00:05:29,314 --> 00:05:34,407
We generated five thousand Indian adults:
three thousand with Type 2 diabetes,

83
00:05:34,457 --> 00:05:38,264
one thousand with prediabetes and
one thousand without diabetes.

84
00:05:38,314 --> 00:05:44,264
Each has demographics, lifestyle and a
regional diet, rice in the south and east,

85
00:05:44,314 --> 00:05:45,979
wheat in the north.

86
00:05:46,029 --> 00:05:48,550
They have diagnoses coded in ICD-10,

87
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:52,836
three years of lab results
and vitals coded in LOINC,

88
00:05:52,886 --> 00:05:57,121
and medicines that follow a realistic
treatment ladder: metformin first,

89
00:05:57,171 --> 00:06:00,550
then a second drug,
then insulin if needed.

90
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:05,693
We also added genetic markers: four
known diabetes risk genes, like TCF7L2,

91
00:06:05,743 --> 00:06:07,836
and a polygenic risk score.

92
00:06:07,886 --> 00:06:10,836
The dynamic stream is the wearable data.

93
00:06:10,886 --> 00:06:15,979
For every patient we simulated fourteen days
at five-minute resolution: CGM glucose,

94
00:06:16,029 --> 00:06:19,407
heart rate, heart-rate variability,
steps and sleep stages.

95
00:06:19,457 --> 00:06:22,836
That is about twenty
million readings per stream.

96
00:06:22,886 --> 00:06:28,407
On top of that, an app log of
meals, medicine doses and exercise.

97
00:06:28,457 --> 00:06:31,407
We made it feel Indian and real.

98
00:06:31,457 --> 00:06:37,836
Breakfast around 8:30, lunch around 1:30,
chai at 5, and a late dinner around 9.

99
00:06:37,886 --> 00:06:39,979
Idli, dosa, rajma-chawal,
roti-sabzi, biryani.

100
00:06:40,029 --> 00:06:42,979
Wedding feasts, fasting
days, illness, missed doses.

101
00:06:43,029 --> 00:06:48,979
And just like real patients, people only
log about sixty percent of their meals.

102
00:06:51,029 --> 00:06:54,836
The key design idea is
one body, two streams.

103
00:06:54,886 --> 00:07:00,407
Both streams are generated from the same
hidden physiology for each person: how

104
00:07:00,457 --> 00:07:04,693
sensitive they are to insulin,
how well their pancreas works,

105
00:07:04,743 --> 00:07:07,264
how regularly they take their medicines.

106
00:07:07,314 --> 00:07:12,407
So the lab HbA1c and the CGM
curve agree with each other.

107
00:07:12,457 --> 00:07:18,836
The HbA1c estimated from the CGM matches
the lab HbA1c with a correlation of 0.93.

108
00:07:18,886 --> 00:07:25,264
And these hidden values are never given to
the models, so the models cannot cheat.

109
00:07:25,314 --> 00:07:26,550
Then we validated.

110
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:32,121
The health record matches Indian studies:
average HbA1c of 7.8 percent in diabetics,

111
00:07:32,171 --> 00:07:35,550
45 percent with hypertension,
89 percent on metformin.

112
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:38,550
All 14 of our literature checks passed.

113
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:42,407
The wearable data passed
all 16 of its checks.

114
00:07:42,457 --> 00:07:45,836
Most importantly, we compared
it with real patients.

115
00:07:45,886 --> 00:07:47,121
We used ShanghaiT2DM,

116
00:07:47,171 --> 00:07:52,693
a public dataset of real CGM recordings
from people with Type 2 diabetes.

117
00:07:52,743 --> 00:07:55,264
At the same average glucose level,

118
00:07:55,314 --> 00:08:00,836
real patients spend 83.5 percent of
their time in range; ours spend 82.3.

119
00:08:00,886 --> 00:08:04,264
Time above 180: 14.6
percent real, 14.9 ours.

120
00:08:04,314 --> 00:08:09,407
And after a meal, glucose peaks
about 90 minutes later in both.

121
00:08:09,457 --> 00:08:12,836
Our meals cause bigger
rises, which is expected,

122
00:08:12,886 --> 00:08:18,407
because rice and roti meals carry more
carbohydrate than the small hospital meals

123
00:08:18,457 --> 00:08:19,693
in that study.

124
00:08:21,743 --> 00:08:22,979
Next, the models.

125
00:08:23,029 --> 00:08:29,407
We used two kinds of twin, because we
found that one kind cannot do everything.

126
00:08:29,457 --> 00:08:31,121
The first kind predicts.

127
00:08:31,171 --> 00:08:36,264
Every thirty minutes, we build one
row per patient with 97 features.

128
00:08:36,314 --> 00:08:40,979
These include the last six
hours of glucose and its trend,

129
00:08:41,029 --> 00:08:45,693
what this patient's glucose
usually does at this time of day,

130
00:08:45,743 --> 00:08:50,407
logged meals and doses, steps,
heart rate and last night's sleep,

131
00:08:50,457 --> 00:08:54,264
plus the health record: HbA1c, kidney
function, medicines, genetics.

132
00:08:54,314 --> 00:08:56,836
That gives us 3.2 million rows.

133
00:08:56,886 --> 00:09:01,550
We split them by patient: 70
percent of patients for training,

134
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:05,407
15 percent for tuning, and
15 percent, 750 patients,

135
00:09:05,457 --> 00:09:07,550
kept completely unseen for testing.

136
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:13,979
On this data we trained LightGBM, seven
models: three alerts, for a spike above 180,

137
00:09:14,029 --> 00:09:21,264
above 250 and a low below 70, and four
forecasts, 30, 60, 90 and 120 minutes ahead.

138
00:09:21,314 --> 00:09:26,407
LightGBM also tells us why it made
each prediction, through SHAP values,

139
00:09:26,457 --> 00:09:28,979
which we turn into plain-language reasons.

140
00:09:29,029 --> 00:09:34,979
We also trained a GRU, a deep-learning
model that reads the raw six-hour sequence,

141
00:09:35,029 --> 00:09:39,264
eleven channels every five minutes,
together with the health record.

142
00:09:39,314 --> 00:09:43,550
It trains on a laptop GPU
in about seven minutes.

143
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:47,836
The final prediction is the
simple average of both models,

144
00:09:47,886 --> 00:09:50,836
with nothing tuned on the test patients.

145
00:09:50,886 --> 00:09:53,407
The second kind of twin simulates.

146
00:09:53,457 --> 00:10:00,264
During testing we discovered something
important: if we add a meal or a walk to the

147
00:10:00,314 --> 00:10:02,836
inputs, the machine-learning
models barely react.

148
00:10:02,886 --> 00:10:05,836
They learned correlation,
not cause and effect.

149
00:10:05,886 --> 00:10:11,407
So for what-if questions we built a
personal physiology twin: equations for how

150
00:10:11,457 --> 00:10:15,264
food is absorbed and how
glucose comes back down,

151
00:10:15,314 --> 00:10:21,264
with three settings learned for each
patient from their own first week: how much

152
00:10:21,314 --> 00:10:25,550
sugar rises per portion of
carbohydrate, how quickly it recovers,

153
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:27,693
and how much walking helps.

154
00:10:27,743 --> 00:10:32,407
The machine-learning models answer "what
will happen?" The physiology twin answers

155
00:10:32,457 --> 00:10:34,550
"what if we change something?"

156
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:41,264
Now let me show you how
a doctor would use this.

157
00:10:43,314 --> 00:10:45,836
First, the strip at the top.

158
00:10:45,886 --> 00:10:48,407
This prototype replays
fourteen recorded days,

159
00:10:48,457 --> 00:10:53,550
so we can move through time
and watch the twin at work.

160
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:58,264
In a real clinic, this would
simply be the live clock.

161
00:10:58,314 --> 00:11:04,264
Right now it is Saturday, the 26th of
September, at 7:30 in the evening.

162
00:11:06,314 --> 00:11:09,693
On the left is the doctor's patient list.

163
00:11:09,743 --> 00:11:11,836
751 patients are being monitored.

164
00:11:11,886 --> 00:11:19,121
55 are urgent: a low, or a reading above
250, is likely in the next two hours.

165
00:11:19,171 --> 00:11:23,407
58 are on watch: a spike
above 180 is likely.

166
00:11:23,457 --> 00:11:29,407
The list is sorted by risk, so the
doctor always starts at the top.

167
00:11:31,457 --> 00:11:33,121
Take this patient, P00152.

168
00:11:33,171 --> 00:11:36,121
Her glucose is 93, which looks fine.

169
00:11:36,171 --> 00:11:39,121
But the twin says low sugar likely.

170
00:11:39,171 --> 00:11:40,321
Why?

171
00:11:40,371 --> 00:11:47,179
Her glucose has dropped about 60 points
in the last two hours and is lower than

172
00:11:47,229 --> 00:11:49,750
usual for this time of day.

173
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:56,179
Its suggestion is simple: take 15 grams
of fast sugar and recheck in 15 minutes.

174
00:11:56,229 --> 00:11:59,607
That is a low caught before it happens.

175
00:12:01,657 --> 00:12:07,607
This patient is the opposite: already at
242, with a reading above 250 likely.

176
00:12:07,657 --> 00:12:11,893
Two patients, two very different
problems, both visible in seconds.

177
00:12:13,943 --> 00:12:16,036
Now our demo patient, Sunita.

178
00:12:16,086 --> 00:12:19,893
She is 48, from South India,
eats rice-based meals,

179
00:12:19,943 --> 00:12:23,750
has had diabetes for three
years, and takes metformin,

180
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:26,321
glimepiride and two more
diabetes medicines.

181
00:12:26,371 --> 00:12:32,321
She was created separately, outside our
dataset, so no model has ever seen her.

182
00:12:34,371 --> 00:12:37,321
This clinical summary is
the thirty-second read.

183
00:12:37,371 --> 00:12:42,036
On the left: right now her
glucose is 123 and steady,

184
00:12:42,086 --> 00:12:46,750
but it could go above 180
in the next two hours,

185
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:51,464
because it usually peaks near
220 after this time of day.

186
00:12:51,514 --> 00:12:56,179
On the right are the findings
from the last fourteen days,

187
00:12:56,229 --> 00:13:01,750
and the first one matters most:
low sugar ten percent of the time,

188
00:13:01,800 --> 00:13:06,464
against a target of under four
percent, mostly in the morning.

189
00:13:06,514 --> 00:13:11,607
The twin suggests reviewing her glimepiride,
a medicine known to cause lows.

190
00:13:11,657 --> 00:13:19,321
It also flags that only 54 percent of her
time is in range and that only about half

191
00:13:19,371 --> 00:13:22,321
her doses were confirmed in the app.

192
00:13:22,371 --> 00:13:27,036
A doctor sees all of that
without opening a single chart.

193
00:13:29,086 --> 00:13:35,464
Here is her glucose over the last twelve
hours: the black line is the CGM.

194
00:13:35,514 --> 00:13:41,036
The blue dashed line is the twin's
forecast for the next two hours,

195
00:13:41,086 --> 00:13:44,464
and the shaded band is the likely range.

196
00:13:44,514 --> 00:13:48,321
The circles are meals she
logged, with their carbs,

197
00:13:48,371 --> 00:13:52,607
and the dots at the top
are her medicine doses.

198
00:13:52,657 --> 00:13:56,036
You can hover over any marker for details.

199
00:13:58,086 --> 00:14:00,179
Because this is a replay,

200
00:14:00,229 --> 00:14:05,750
we can do something a real clinic cannot:
reveal what actually happened next.

201
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:09,607
The grey line is the real
glucose after "now",

202
00:14:09,657 --> 00:14:13,036
and you can see the forecast followed it.

203
00:14:15,086 --> 00:14:16,750
Now watch the replay.

204
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:19,750
Time moves forward thirty
minutes every second.

205
00:14:19,800 --> 00:14:24,036
Keep an eye on the high-sugar
card on the right.

206
00:14:24,086 --> 00:14:30,036
It turns from unlikely, to possible, to
likely, before the glucose line crosses 180.

207
00:14:30,086 --> 00:14:33,893
That is the early warning
the challenge asked for.

208
00:14:35,943 --> 00:14:40,607
The reasons come from the model
itself, rewritten in plain language.

209
00:14:40,657 --> 00:14:47,036
For example: "usually peaks near 220 after
this time of day", and "high for hours".

210
00:14:47,086 --> 00:14:50,893
Only reasons a doctor
can act on are shown.

211
00:14:52,943 --> 00:14:54,607
Now the personal twin.

212
00:14:54,657 --> 00:14:58,036
For Sunita it suggests: at the next meal,

213
00:14:58,086 --> 00:15:04,893
swap her usual large rice meal for a smaller
low-GI one, like roti, dal and salad.

214
00:15:04,943 --> 00:15:09,607
Her twin estimates that lowers
her peak by about 37 points.

215
00:15:09,657 --> 00:15:14,321
Or walk for twenty minutes,
starting half an hour after eating.

216
00:15:16,371 --> 00:15:19,321
These numbers come from her own data.

217
00:15:19,371 --> 00:15:25,321
Her twin learned that her sugar rises about
17 points for every roti-sized portion

218
00:15:25,371 --> 00:15:27,464
of carbs, more than average,

219
00:15:27,514 --> 00:15:33,036
and takes about four and a half
hours to settle after a meal.

220
00:15:35,086 --> 00:15:39,321
When the doctor clicks "Try it",
the what-if simulator opens.

221
00:15:39,371 --> 00:15:42,321
The dotted line is her usual meal.

222
00:15:42,371 --> 00:15:47,036
The solid line is the same meal
with a twenty-minute walk.

223
00:15:47,086 --> 00:15:54,321
Now I change the food type from rice to
roti and dal, and the peak drops further.

224
00:15:54,371 --> 00:15:58,607
If I mark a skipped
dose, the curve goes up.

225
00:15:58,657 --> 00:16:05,036
The doctor can see the effect of each
choice on this patient's body before giving

226
00:16:05,086 --> 00:16:06,236
the advice.

227
00:16:08,286 --> 00:16:12,950
Below, the doctor has the full health
record: latest labs, medicines,

228
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:18,521
diagnoses and her HbA1c trend over three
years, and her genetic risk markers.

229
00:16:20,571 --> 00:16:24,379
And there is a dark
theme, for night shifts.

230
00:16:26,429 --> 00:16:28,521
Now, how good is it?

231
00:16:28,571 --> 00:16:34,521
Every number here is on the 750 test
patients that the models never saw.

232
00:16:34,571 --> 00:16:41,807
For the main alert, a spike above 180 in
the next two hours, the AUROC is 0.958.

233
00:16:41,857 --> 00:16:46,521
A simple rule that just follows
the current trend gets 0.71.

234
00:16:46,571 --> 00:16:51,236
For diabetics only, which is
the harder group, it is 0.924.

235
00:16:51,286 --> 00:16:55,093
95 percent of spikes were
flagged before they happened.

236
00:16:55,143 --> 00:16:59,807
When we set the alert to
catch 80 percent of spikes,

237
00:16:59,857 --> 00:17:02,807
three out of four alerts are correct,

238
00:17:02,857 --> 00:17:07,521
at about one and a half
alerts per patient per day.

239
00:17:07,571 --> 00:17:10,950
For lows below 70, the AUROC is 0.978,

240
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,807
and 94.5 percent of lows
were flagged in advance.

241
00:17:14,857 --> 00:17:20,379
For the glucose forecast one hour
ahead, the average error is 13.8 mg/dL.

242
00:17:20,429 --> 00:17:25,521
If you simply assume glucose
stays the same, the error is 21.3.

243
00:17:25,571 --> 00:17:28,093
At two hours: 17.1 against 31.7.

244
00:17:30,143 --> 00:17:33,093
Does fusing the two streams actually help?

245
00:17:33,143 --> 00:17:34,379
We tested it.

246
00:17:34,429 --> 00:17:37,807
CGM alone gives a spike AUROC of 0.952.

247
00:17:37,857 --> 00:17:43,807
Adding the smartwatch and app logs raises
it, adding the health record raises it,

248
00:17:43,857 --> 00:17:47,236
and the full fusion is best, at 0.956,

249
00:17:47,286 --> 00:17:51,093
with the one-hour error
falling from 14.8 to 13.9.

250
00:17:51,143 --> 00:17:55,807
The gains are modest, because
the CGM is the strongest signal,

251
00:17:55,857 --> 00:17:57,950
but each stream adds something.

252
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,236
For lows, the CGM alone
does almost all the work.

253
00:18:04,286 --> 00:18:06,379
And does personal beat average?

254
00:18:06,429 --> 00:18:10,664
When the twin predicts the
rise after a brand-new meal,

255
00:18:10,714 --> 00:18:16,236
using what it learned in the
first week, its error is 25 mg/dL,

256
00:18:16,286 --> 00:18:18,807
against 31 for a one-size-fits-all model.

257
00:18:18,857 --> 00:18:23,950
Its learned carb sensitivity matches
each patient's hidden true value with a

258
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:25,236
correlation of 0.92.

259
00:18:27,286 --> 00:18:31,521
We also tested it the way it will be used.

260
00:18:31,571 --> 00:18:36,664
On a new user's first day, without
meal logging, without a smartwatch,

261
00:18:36,714 --> 00:18:42,236
and with a noisier sensor, the spike
AUROC stays between 0.94 and 0.96.

262
00:18:42,286 --> 00:18:48,236
That means the system can work with
just a CGM, which matters in India,

263
00:18:48,286 --> 00:18:50,807
where not everyone has a smartwatch.

264
00:18:50,857 --> 00:18:55,950
We also ran 24 clinical scenario checks,
including feasts, missed doses, illness,

265
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,521
fasting on sulfonylureas
and night-time lows.

266
00:18:58,571 --> 00:18:59,721
19 passed.

267
00:18:59,771 --> 00:19:04,436
The 5 that failed were what-if checks
on the machine-learning models,

268
00:19:04,486 --> 00:19:09,579
and that failure is exactly why the
physiology twin answers what-if questions

269
00:19:09,629 --> 00:19:10,779
instead.

270
00:19:10,829 --> 00:19:15,493
One more finding : knowing every
past meal perfectly barely helps.

271
00:19:15,543 --> 00:19:19,350
The biggest gain comes from
knowing the next meal.

272
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:22,779
If a patient shares a simple meal plan,

273
00:19:22,829 --> 00:19:27,064
the spike AUROC for diabetics
rises from 0.92 to 0.96.

274
00:19:29,114 --> 00:19:32,493
I want to be honest about the limits.

275
00:19:32,543 --> 00:19:35,064
First, all our data is synthetic.

276
00:19:35,114 --> 00:19:38,493
We validated it carefully,
including against real patients,

277
00:19:38,543 --> 00:19:44,064
but accuracy on real patients will
be lower than what you have seen.

278
00:19:44,114 --> 00:19:49,636
Second, the physiology twin uses the
same kind of equations as our simulator,

279
00:19:49,686 --> 00:19:54,779
so how well it recovers each
patient's physiology is a best case.

280
00:19:54,829 --> 00:19:55,979
Third,

281
00:19:56,029 --> 00:20:01,979
the low-sugar alert is sensitive but
not precise: about three out of four low

282
00:20:02,029 --> 00:20:03,693
alerts are false alarms.

283
00:20:03,743 --> 00:20:10,121
For lows, we chose to warn early rather
than miss one, but this needs work.

284
00:20:10,171 --> 00:20:15,693
Our next steps: validate on real,
consented CGM data with a partner hospital.

285
00:20:15,743 --> 00:20:19,979
Keep re-fitting each patient's
twin as new meals are logged.

286
00:20:20,029 --> 00:20:24,693
Build a patient app with an
"I'm about to eat" button,

287
00:20:24,743 --> 00:20:28,121
which our experiments show
improves predictions the most.

288
00:20:28,171 --> 00:20:34,121
And extend the same fusion of health
records and wearables to blood pressure and

289
00:20:34,171 --> 00:20:35,321
heart risk.

290
00:20:37,371 --> 00:20:43,321
A doctor with hundreds of patients has
two questions every morning: who needs me

291
00:20:43,371 --> 00:20:46,321
now, and what should I tell them?

292
00:20:46,371 --> 00:20:50,607
Sugar Twin India answers both,
two hours before the event,

293
00:20:50,657 --> 00:20:54,036
with advice tested on
each patient's own twin.

294
00:20:54,086 --> 00:20:59,607
Everything, the data, the models, the
dashboard and every result you have seen,

295
00:20:59,657 --> 00:21:02,607
is open source on GitHub at
github.com/Aayushg0908/sugar-twin-india.

296
00:21:02,657 --> 00:21:04,321
Thank you for watching.
